{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-error-recovery", "file": "references/multi-rejection-demo-discipline.md", "content": "# Multi-rejection discipline for demo-driven ARC

In a task with one or more incorrect submissions, an incorrect result falsifies the whole proposed generator. Do not resubmit a near-identical grid or make an evidence-free palette/coordinate tweak. Rebuild a ledger from every input-output pair, including unchanged cells, and separately test scope, geometry, mapping, and palette.

When multiple candidate grids have failed and the remaining evidence does not determine the test output, stop guessing. State internally at least two concrete hypotheses and the specific observation that distinguishes them. Request one demo only if it can supply that observation, then integrate it before the next action; never treat receiving a demo as an automatic reason for another request. Submit only when one candidate explains every pair and unambiguously yields the test grid. Keep each turn to one action-only JSON object.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-seq0/frozen/held-out/play/users/u034/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-error-recovery/references/multi-rejection-demo-discipline.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-demo-checkpoint", "file": "references/separator-vs-motif-check.md", "content": "# Full-width separator versus salient interior motif

When a test grid has a complete horizontal/vertical line alongside a conspicuous patterned region, don't select a transform just because the motif is visually rich. Start from exact input/output diffs in every demo. Record separator coordinates and whether separator cells change, plus every changed isolated marker and unchanged lookalikes. Compare candidate rules that operate on the separator/bands against rules operating on the motif, and require a single candidate to explain all positive and negative cells before projecting to the test.

In this instance, a minimal demo changed a sparse marker and recolored the adjacent full-width row below a full-width divider; another recolored the row immediately above a full-width divider while preserving a lone marker. The test had a bottom full-width color-2 row and an unrelated dense color-1 patch. The initial guess recolored the row above the divider and copied the patch as if it were a template; it was rejected. Do not infer a color-8 band merely by mirroring the first demo's local row offset. Need more discriminating evidence to establish which band relative to divider is selected and what sparse markers indicate.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-seq0/frozen/held-out/play/users/u034/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-demo-checkpoint/references/separator-vs-motif-check.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-demo-checkpoint", "file": "references/sparse-geometry-vs-template.md", "content": "# Sparse geometry versus motif copying

When a sparse grid contains a compact colored motif plus isolated same-color marks, the motif may cue a geometric operation rather than serve as a template to replicate. Record the exact motif anchor and every marker coordinate. Test whether aligned markers define endpoints/borders and whether intervening cells are completed; infer eligible orientation, endpoint class, and scope separately. Account for existing markers that remain unchanged. Compare this with literal template-occurrence predictions against every demo, including negative evidence, before acting. A varied second geometry can discriminate the candidate mechanisms; request it only when it could alter the prediction.
