{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-error-recovery", "file": "references/local-demo-global-rule.md", "content": "# Separating a demo's local effect from its global rule

A minimal example may reveal what happens to particular cells without proving how those cells are selected in larger grids. In this session, the 2×10 pair established that a trailing run of 9s becomes 8s and that part of a run remains unchanged, but it did not validate a whole-grid operation for a separate 22×25 test. The first submission simply erased the test's 1-cells; rejection falsified that extrapolation. The next submission erased all test marks except an isolated vertical pair, without a demonstration supporting that selection, and was also unsupported.

When demo and test scales differ sharply, separate and validate: (1) local value mapping, (2) trigger/selection, (3) propagation or geometry, and (4) scope. Keep unchanged demo cells as constraints. If multiple global generators still fit, request a demo designed to distinguish them rather than submitting a conjecture. Never infer an exception mask solely from a visually salient test pattern.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-seq0/frozen/held-out/play/users/u034/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-error-recovery/references/local-demo-global-rule.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-error-recovery", "file": "references/trajectory-after-error.md", "content": "# Revising sparse trajectory hypotheses after a wrong output

A wrong submission that extends only one apparent ray is not repaired by shifting its start or changing a single cell. Re-derive the generator from each demo by recording every seed marker, color, direction vector, and endpoint. In the example reviewed here, one demo was identity (a sparse object did not trigger extension), while the larger demo showed simultaneous rays: a 7-ray extended down-right from its marker, and 6-ray(s) extended along the other diagonal direction from multiple marker locations. The test had several 9 markers plus paired 7 and 6 markers; assuming the salient 9-marker cluster was irrelevant and extending just one 6 ray / one short 7 ray was unsupported and was rejected. General lesson: compare the full input geometry to positive and negative demos; infer whether multiple marker colors or every compatible seed launches a ray, and project each ray through the full grid to the evidenced boundary. Preserve original objects and background. Do not submit a one-color or one-ray interpretation unless it accounts for all trajectory behavior in the demonstrations.

This example is not a universal rule for launching rays from all markers: use it to formulate competing hypotheses, then select the one that explains the complete demos and test configuration.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-seq0/frozen/held-out/play/users/u034/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-error-recovery/references/trajectory-after-error.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-mixed-demo-review", "file": "references/seed-motif-generator-checkpoint.md", "content": "# Seed-motif generator checkpoint after a wrong submission

A salient colored seed embedded in a noisy grid does not by itself imply a copy rule. Candidate classes can include translated shape occurrences, periodic/lattice replication, directional rays, or combinations. Before extrapolating, record the seed's exact coordinates and the complete input/output difference mask in every demo. Verify both positive and negative cases: why each changed cell is selected, and why visually similar unchanged cells are excluded. Check palettes and dimensions that vary across demos; distinguish the seed color, target/background color, and unrelated noise empirically rather than assuming one role from a single pair.

After an incorrect submission, discard the whole generator. Do not move a few cells, swap a presumed background color, or switch to a nearby geometric interpretation without replaying it against every demo. With a costly attempt budget, use remaining demos for explicit competing hypotheses and only submit after a full-mask validation. Preserve all cells outside the supported transformation and verify dimensions/row lengths.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-seq0/frozen/held-out/play/users/u034/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-mixed-demo-review/references/seed-motif-generator-checkpoint.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-demo-checkpoint", "file": "references/post-error-fill-scope-review.md", "content": "# Post-error fill scope review

This stream contained one demo in which gaps between aligned markers were colored, another where the same kind of candidate rule was not evident, an identity example featuring a closed rectangular outline, and a test with several tempting but structurally different boundary patterns. Two speculative fill submissions failed. The durable lesson is to separate (1) whether the geometry is aligned/enclosed, (2) what configuration triggers action, and (3) which exact cells are in scope. An identity example is strong negative evidence against indiscriminately filling every enclosure or same-row/column gap.

After a rejected fill, reconstruct all demo diffs and explicitly compare gap-filling, enclosure-filling, and identity-preservation hypotheses against every pair. Do not infer the correct mask from a prior session note; verify the current grid's boundaries, coordinates, and dimensions. Spend another demo only after naming competing hypotheses and the specific outcome that would distinguish them. Treat a previous session's noted correction as a candidate to verify, never as a substitute for current-instance evidence.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-seq0/frozen/held-out/play/users/u034/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-demo-checkpoint/references/post-error-fill-scope-review.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-mixed-demo-review", "file": "references/cellwise-mask-and-generator-validation.md", "content": "# Cellwise mask validation across heterogeneous demos

When an ARC task has a distinctive colored seed in noisy grids, do not equate resemblance with the rule. Track the seed shape, eligible target values, and spatial generator as independent hypotheses. For every demo, enumerate the full input/output difference mask and preserve negative evidence: unchanged lookalikes, untouched target-valued cells, and the original seed.

Before another demo or a submission, replay the proposed generator against every pair, especially when seed shape, palette, or dimensions vary. A literal translated-template matcher that looks plausible on one example but fails the varied demos is falsified; do not patch it by shifting a few cells. Request additional examples only for explicit remaining alternatives and a specific discriminating observation.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-seq0/frozen/held-out/play/users/u034/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-mixed-demo-review/references/cellwise-mask-and-generator-validation.md"}

