{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-demo-checkpoint", "file": "references/session-consecutive-demo-synthesis.md", "content": "# Consecutive demo requests without synthesis

In this instance, five demo requests were made in succession across turns, despite each new pair changing the evidence materially. The examples looked unlike each other locally, but their salient relation was a long straight divider/marker line in color 2 plus an object on one side. In demonstrations, the output added a parallel line of color 8 on the opposite side of the object/partition, preserving the original line and object. The test similarly has a vertical 2 line at column 14 and a compact 0 motif immediately to its left; this suggests completing a parallel 8 line at column 11. This is the kind of structural synthesis that should happen after each pair, not after exhausting several demos.

Operational lesson: after every returned demo, record divider orientation/location, side containing the object, output line orientation/location, and preserved cells. Compare candidate relations across all prior examples. Do not issue the next request unless a specific unresolved fork remains and a new pair could distinguish it. A repeated pattern of asking for demos without stating the evolving hypotheses spends budget without increasing useful evidence.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-seq0/frozen/held-out/play/users/u034/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-demo-checkpoint/references/session-consecutive-demo-synthesis.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-error-recovery", "file": "references/payload-validity-feedback.md", "content": "# Payload validity versus rule feedback

In Continual-ARC, feedback may explicitly identify a submission as an invalid ARC grid rather than simply incorrect. Keep these diagnoses separate. Before interpreting invalid-grid feedback as evidence against the transformation, mechanically check expected row count, each row's exact width, cell values (integers 0–9), and dimension limits. Long handwritten JSON payloads are particularly prone to one malformed row. A payload-valid but incorrect result falsifies the proposed generator; rebuild it from all demos rather than making cosmetic color/placement edits. After repeated failures, stop speculative submissions and use another demo only when it distinguishes concrete remaining hypotheses.
